<h2>The allegation and the family dimension</h2>
<p>The FIR alleged that the petitioner's father, the main accused, received Rs 6,10,000 from three persons on the promise of selling land to them; the sale never materialised and the money was not returned. The petitioner was named alongside his father. His counsel pressed false implication: the transaction, the promise and the money trail all centred on the father, and the son's inclusion reflected the familiar pattern of arraying family members in economic FIRs arising out of soured land deals.</p>
<p>Two features of the record did the heavy lifting. First, the father himself — the person to whom the money was allegedly paid — had been granted anticipatory bail on 25 November 2025 in A.B.A. No. 2183 of 2025. Second, two similarly placed co-accused had been protected on 28 July 2026 in A.B.A. No. 4144 of 2026. Against that backdrop, denying protection to the son would have inverted the logic of the earlier orders.</p>

<h2>The order</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Protection:</strong> on arrest or surrender within three weeks, release on bail bonds of Rs 30,000 with two sureties of the like amount, before the Judicial Magistrate First Class, Latehar.</li>
  <li><strong>Undertaking:</strong> the petitioner is bound by his undertaking to cooperate with the ongoing investigation.</li>
  <li><strong>Statutory conditions:</strong> the conditions under Section 482(2) of the BNSS, 2023 apply — availability for interrogation, no tampering with witnesses, no leaving India without permission.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Parity as a working tool, not a formula</h2>
<p>Parity operates differently in anticipatory bail than in regular bail, because the question is not who should be released but who needs to be arrested at all. Where the principal accused is already protected, the case for custodial interrogation of a secondary accused — particularly a relative whose alleged role is derivative — weakens considerably. Courts remain alive to the counter-scenario: parity fails where the applicant has a distinct, aggravated role, where he is the moving spirit of the fraud, or where his conduct after the FIR (evasion, tampering) distinguishes him from the protected co-accused. None of those features was shown here, and the State's opposition did not identify any role-specific material against the son.</p>

<h2>What practitioners should take from the order</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Point of attention</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Drafting the application</td><td>Place the co-accused orders on record with case numbers and dates; parity arguments fail when the earlier orders are described loosely</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Role comparison</td><td>Show that the applicant's attributed role is the same as, or lesser than, that of the protected co-accused</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Undertaking</td><td>A recorded undertaking to cooperate with the investigation often tips the balance in economic offence matters</td></tr>
  <tr><td>After the order</td><td>Honour every condition scrupulously — breach invites cancellation and colours any future application</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Complainants in failed land transactions should remember that anticipatory bail neither ends the prosecution nor touches the civil remedy for recovery of the money. A parallel civil suit for refund, with interest, often proves the more direct route to the actual objective — return of the consideration.</p>
</div>
